      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Attached raw PDF page 30, printed page 8: the opening clause of paragraph two says variable-coefficient hyperbolic equations may lack conservation form. Pages 23 and 25–27 supply the scalar, hyperbolic, flux, and integral context.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation asserts existence of a scalar coefficient with a pointwise real eigenbasis and no universal flux representation.",
      "reasoning": "The comparison concerns only the selected mathematical clause, excluding the subsequent discussion of numerical methods. The supplied hashes are identifiers; they were not independently recomputed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Page 23 introduces real states on real space and time and explicitly includes the scalar case m = 1.",
      "translation_evidence": "The coefficient and profiles are real-valued functions on ℝ; the matrix acts on Fin 1 → ℝ; the flux has two real arguments.",
      "reasoning": "These objects describe an admissible scalar subclass. The second flux argument is the state and the first is position, enlarging the candidate flux class beyond the displayed f(q)."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected clause uses 'may not', expressing possibility rather than universal nonconservation.",
      "translation_evidence": "One coefficient exists with all four conjuncts; no single flux works for all profiles and points. Eigenbasis witnesses may depend on position.",
      "reasoning": "The existential force is preserved. A flux representation of an equation must work uniformly across profiles, so its placement before the profile quantifiers is appropriate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected class is hyperbolic with variable coefficients; no positivity or smoothness restriction is stated.",
      "translation_evidence": "The existential coefficient is C∞ and strictly positive, and each scalar matrix has a real eigenbasis.",
      "reasoning": "Smoothness and positivity strengthen the properties of an existential witness rather than restrict an outer universal theorem. Variability follows from flux nonexistence: a constant coefficient c admits F(x,z) = cz."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The entire selected conclusion is that conservation form is not guaranteed for variable-coefficient hyperbolic equations.",
      "translation_evidence": "The same coefficient satisfies hyperbolicity and the negation of a universal spatial flux identity.",
      "reasoning": "This supplies a counterexample to the claimed automatic implication. No conclusion from the excluded remainder of the paragraph is needed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Page 25 defines hyperbolicity through a real eigenbasis and displays q_t + ∂_x f(q) = 0; page 26 gives its interval balance.",
      "translation_evidence": "D001 and D003 describe the complete scalar eigenbasis condition. D002 and D038 describe actual derivatives of y ↦ F(y,u(y)), including positional dependence. D006, D023, D024, and D027 identify C∞ rather than analyticity.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied ledger consistently interprets the operators and supporting real algebraic and topological structures. For q_t + a(x)q_x = 0, the denied identity is precisely the spatial operator identity required for conservation form in the same dependent variable."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source makes an unqualified possibility claim without a specified witness class.",
      "translation_evidence": "The counterexample is additionally scalar, globally smooth, and positive, and excludes explicitly position-dependent fluxes.",
      "reasoning": "These are genuine additional existential guarantees. Negating existence of a flux from the enlarged candidate class also excludes the source's state-only fluxes."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "In equations (1.8)–(1.10), q is the conserved state and f is its flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "The profile u supplies the state argument of F; a is the coefficient; [a(x)] is the hyperbolicity matrix.",
      "reasoning": "The roles remain distinct. The statement concerns conservation of the original state and makes no assertion excluding a transformed dependent variable or integrating factor."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Page 23 explicitly permits dimension one; the selected clause specifies no coefficient formula or numerical constants.",
      "translation_evidence": "Fin 1 fixes the dimension to one, positivity uses real zero, and a(x) multiplies the profile derivative.",
      "reasoning": "The constants and singleton indices have their stated meanings. No particular coefficient formula is attributed to the source."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected class broadly includes variable-coefficient first-order hyperbolic equations.",
      "translation_evidence": "The witness belongs to the scalar, spatially varying, positive C∞ subclass on ℝ.",
      "reasoning": "For an existential counterexample, obtaining a witness in a narrower admissible subclass strengthens the result. It does not omit cases of a universally quantified source theorem."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Pages 26–27 emphasize that failure of classical differentiability at discontinuities does not itself destroy integral conservation.",
      "translation_evidence": "The coefficient is smooth; constant and affine profiles are included in the universal derivative condition; derivatives use full real neighborhoods.",
      "reasoning": "The obstruction can already be detected using smooth profiles, so it is not an artifact of discontinuities or undefined derivatives. There are no endpoint or empty-dimensional cases."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source asserts actual possibility of nonconservation in a nonempty hyperbolic class.",
      "translation_evidence": "The statement has no outer premises. Affine profiles realize every state and slope; the one-dimensional eigenbasis condition is automatic.",
      "reasoning": "For example, a(x) = 1 + x² is a positive smooth nonconstant coefficient. If a candidate F satisfied the property, constant profiles would force F(x,z) to be independent of x for each z. Writing it as f(z), affine profiles would then force f′(z) = a(x) for every x and z, contradicting nonconstancy. This example establishes nonvacuity without attributing a witness to the source."
    },
    {
