      "translation_evidence": "Existence concerns the coefficient; nonexistence concerns a universal flux. A complete eigenbasis and coefficient smoothness are explicit.",
      "reasoning": "These existence claims preserve their roles. No PDE solution-existence or well-posedness theorem is silently substituted or required."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "For the translated witness, consider q_t + a(x)q_x = 0 in the original scalar dependent variable. Its coefficient is smooth and its one-by-one matrix is hyperbolic. The coefficient cannot be constant, since a constant c admits F(x,z) = cz. Any source-style flux f(q) representing this spatial operator would provide F(x,z) = f(z) satisfying the denied derivative identity. Thus the translation supplies a variable-coefficient hyperbolic equation lacking the source's conservation form. The obstruction is visible on constant and affine smooth profiles, so it does not confuse failure of classical regularity with absence of integral conservation."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "As a statement of what the selected source assertion guarantees, the broad possibility claim does not supply a scalar counterexample on all of ℝ with a positive C∞ coefficient, nor does it explicitly exclude every position-dependent flux. Those additional guarantees require mathematical construction and argument beyond the selected assertion. They are compatible, nonvacuous strengthenings, not explicit source conclusions."
    }
  },
  "classification": "faithful-stronger",
  "accepted": true,
  "requires_adjudication": false,
  "findings": [
    {
