    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "conservation-representation-scope",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.8) uses f(q), and equation (1.10) describes conservation of that same state through endpoint fluxes.",
      "translation_evidence": "The negated flux class permits F(x,q), with the full derivative through both arguments.",
      "impact": "Excluding this larger flux class implies exclusion of the displayed source form. Neither the translation nor this judgment asserts impossibility after changing the conserved dependent variable."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The attached primary-source pages support an existential reading of the selected clause in the original state variable. The translation gives a genuine smooth scalar counterexample criterion: its universal flux property forces the coefficient to be constant, whereas flux nonexistence forces variability. Positivity, smoothness, scalar dimension, and the enlarged excluded flux class provide additional substantive guarantees. The result is therefore faithful-stronger."
